.\" umem(1) -- runtime introspection for libumem-using processes.
.TH UMEM 1 "2025-01" "libumem" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
umem \- runtime introspection for libumem-using processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B umem
.B \-\-pid
.I PID
.I CMD
.RI [ ARGS... ]
.br
.B umem
.B \-\-core
.I CORE
.B \-\-exe
.I BIN
.I CMD
.RI [ ARGS... ]
.br
.B umem
.B \-\-dump
.I FILE
.I CMD
.RI [ ARGS... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B umem
is a command-line front-end for libumem's runtime introspection API.
It surfaces the same information that
.BR mdb (1)
exposes on Solaris through the
.BR ::findleaks ", " ::umem_log ", " ::umastat ", " ::whatis ", and"
.B ::bufctl_audit
dcmds.
.PP
Internally,
.B umem
drives
.BR gdb (1)
in batch mode against the target process or core file and invokes the
exported
.BR umem_inspect (3)
entry points (\fBumem_findleaks\fR, \fBumem_log_dump\fR,
\fBumem_status_dump\fR, \fBumem_whatis\fR, \fBumem_walk_dump\fR) using
the debugger's expression evaluator.  In offline mode
.RB ( \-\-dump )
it bypasses the debugger and reads a binary snapshot directly via the
\fBumem_dump_reader\fR(1) helper.
.SH MODES
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Attach to a live process.  The process must be linked against
\fBlibumem\fR.  The target is paused for the duration of each
sub-command and detached afterwards.
.TP
\fB\-\-core\fR \fICORE\fR \fB\-\-exe\fR \fIBIN\fR
Read a core dump.  Requires the original binary so the debugger can
resolve symbols.  Read-only; no side effects on disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump " FILE"
Read a binary snapshot previously written by
\fBumem_inspect_snapshot\fR(3) (extension
.IR .ums ", " .umsnap ", or " .bin ).
The original process is not required; this mode parses the file
directly with the
.BR umem_dump_reader (1)
helper.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBfindleaks\fR \fB[\-f text|json]\fR \fB[\-n N]\fR
Report buffers that are currently allocated, grouped by allocation
stack-trace fingerprint and ranked by total bytes.  The default report
shows the top 50 leak classes; use \fB\-n\fR to change this.  With
\fB\-f json\fR the output is a single-line JSON object suitable for
piping to
.BR jq (1).
.IP
Allocator-internal caches
.RB ( UMC_NOHASH ", " UMC_QCACHE )
are filtered out, and buffers currently sitting in per-CPU magazines
are subtracted (and reported as
.IR cached_skipped )
so the count reflects actual leaks.
.IP
For stack traces the target must run with
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
in its environment.  Without audit the count is still accurate but no
stacks are recorded.
.TP
\fBlog\fR \fB[\-f text|json]\fR \fB[\-n N]\fR
Dump the allocator transaction log in chronological order.  Each
record represents an alloc or free event with thread, timestamp, and
stack trace.  Requires the target to run with
.BR UMEM_LOGGING=transaction= \fIsize\fR
(for example
.BR transaction=1m ).
.TP
\fBstatus\fR \fB[\-f text|json]\fR
Per-cache summary (mdb's \fB::umastat\fR equivalent): bufsize, buffers
in use, total buffers, memory in use, successful and failed
allocations, depot contention.
.TP
\fBwalk\fR \fB[allocated|freed|log]\fR \fB[\-f text|json]\fR \fB[\-n N]\fR
Stream every entry in the requested set without grouping.  Useful for
custom downstream processing.  Default kind is
.BR allocated .
.TP
\fBwhatis\fR \fIADDR\fR
Resolve an address to its owning cache, slab, and allocation state
(ALLOCATED, FREE, or CACHED).  When stack traces are available, also
prints the alloc/free stack from the bufctl audit record.
.TP
\fBbufctl\fR \fIADDR\fR
Alias for
.BR whatis ;
matches the mdb dcmd name
.BR ::bufctl_audit .
.TP
.BI snapshot " PATH"
Write a snapshot of allocator state to
.IR PATH .
If
.I PATH
ends in
.IR .ums ", " .umsnap ", or " .bin
the v2 binary format is used (parseable offline via
.BR \-\-dump );
otherwise a human-readable text dump is written.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B text
Default; human-readable.  Tables, headers, and stack traces match the
Solaris mdb output closely enough that scripts written for mdb can
often be ported with minimal changes.
.TP
.B json
One JSON object per command, suitable for
.BR jq (1)
or downstream parsing.  Stable schema:
.BR findleaks
emits
.IR total_buffers ", " total_bytes ", " cached_skipped ", " classes[];
.B status
emits
.IR caches[];
.B log
emits
.IR records[];
.B walk
emits
.IR entries[].
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables are read by the target process,
not by
.B umem
itself.  Set them in the target's environment before launch.
.TP
.B UMEM_DEBUG=audit
Record an allocation stack trace for every buffer.  Required for
findleaks to emit stack traces and for whatis to show alloc context.
~30% allocation overhead.
.TP
.BI UMEM_LOGGING=transaction= SIZE
Enable the per-CPU transaction log of size
.IR SIZE
(suffix
.BR k ", " m ", or " g
permitted).  Required for
.B umem log
to return any records.  ~5% allocation overhead.
.TP
.B UMEM_STACKTRACE_ADDR2LINE=1
Opt-in to forking
.BR addr2line (1)
for source-line resolution in stack traces.  Off by default because
the fork interferes with debugger expression evaluation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I tools/gdb/umem_gdb.py
GDB Python extension; sourced automatically by
.BR umem .
Can also be sourced interactively in
.BR gdb (1).
.TP
.I tools/lldb/umem_lldb.py
LLDB equivalent; same command set.
.TP
.I tools/umem_dump_reader
Python reader for v2 binary snapshots (\fI.ums\fR files).  Invoked by
.B umem
in
.B \-\-dump
mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the hottest leak class in a running daemon:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ umem --pid $(pgrep mydaemon) findleaks -n 5
.RE
.fi
.PP
Snapshot state for offline analysis (production-friendly):
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ umem --pid 12345 snapshot /var/log/umem/$(date +%H).ums
$ umem --dump /var/log/umem/14.ums findleaks
.RE
.fi
.PP
Watch leak growth over time:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ while sleep 60; do
>   umem --pid 12345 findleaks -f json |
>     jq -r '"\\(now|todate) total=\\(.total_bytes)"'
> done
.RE
.fi
.PP
Investigate a core dump:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ umem --core core.12345 --exe ./myapp findleaks
$ umem --core core.12345 --exe ./myapp log -n 100
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, non-zero on error (target not running, missing
debugger, malformed snapshot, etc.).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umem_alloc (3),
.BR umem_inspect (3),
.BR umem_debug (3),
.BR umem_debugging (7),
.BR gdb (1),
.BR lldb (1),
.BR mdb (1)
.PP
.I tools/DEBUGGING.md
in the libumem source distribution covers full workflows and
worked examples.
.SH HISTORY
The libumem allocator originated at Sun Microsystems in 2001 (Bonwick
& Adams) as the userspace counterpart to the Solaris kernel's slab
allocator.  Solaris exposed runtime introspection through
.BR mdb (1)
dcmds; this command restores those features on Linux, FreeBSD, and
macOS.
.SH AUTHOR
The umem(1) front-end is part of the libumem revival project; see
.B CHANGELOG.md
for the maintenance history.
